'''''Aloe'' × ''nobilis''''' is a commonly cultivated hybrid aloe in the [[Family (taxonomy)|family]] [[Asphodelaceae]]. It is an artificial hybrid, possibly between ''[[Aloe mitriformis]]'' and ''[[Aloe brevifolia]]''.<ref>{{citation |author1=Smith, G.F|author2=Figueiredo, E.|title=Garden aloes. Growing and breeding cultivars and hybrids |date=2015|publisher=Jacana Media (Pty) Ltd}}</ref><ref name="nob">{{cite journal|title=Notes on ''Aloe'' ×''nobilis'' Haw. (Asphodelaceae: Alooideae) |last1=Smith|first1=G.F.|last2=Figueiredo|first2=E.|year=2015|journal=Haseltonia|volume=21|pages=72–76|doi=10.2985/026.021.0110 }}</ref>

==History==
''Aloe'' × ''nobilis'' was described in 1812 by [[Adrian Hardy Haworth]]. Although both parent species are [[South Africa|South African]], it likely emerged from crosses between cultivated plants in Europe, as it has not been found in the wild in South Africa.<ref name="nob" />

It is one of the most commonly cultivated aloes in Europe, and has become naturalised in parts of Portugal.<ref name="nob" />

